WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.171
(Overlapping voices sing)

00:00:29.568 --> 00:00:31.069
(Singing ends)

00:00:31.528 --> 00:00:34.698
(Applause)

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:37.284
Hi, my name is Holly, and I’m an artist.

00:00:37.325 --> 00:00:38.535
That was my voice,

00:00:38.577 --> 00:00:39.953
but I didn’t sing that clip.

00:00:40.537 --> 00:00:42.581
I trained an AI on my own voice,

00:00:42.581 --> 00:00:45.625
and now she can sing anything
in multiple languages.

00:00:46.251 --> 00:00:48.587
Her name is Holly+,

00:00:48.628 --> 00:00:52.007
and you just heard her perform
“El Cant de la Sibilla,”

00:00:52.048 --> 00:00:55.927
which is a traditional song
arranged by Maria Arnal in Catalan.

00:00:56.303 --> 00:00:58.263
Not a language that I speak

00:00:58.263 --> 00:01:00.974
and not a vocal tradition
that I've trained in.

00:01:01.016 --> 00:01:04.352
Those melismatic runs
are really difficult to hit.

00:01:04.978 --> 00:01:09.107
To show you Holly+'s full range,
I'll also play an example in German.

00:01:09.149 --> 00:01:11.359
This is "Mack the Knife" by Brecht.

00:01:11.985 --> 00:01:15.447
(Singing in German)

00:01:25.332 --> 00:01:26.875
(Music ends)

00:01:28.501 --> 00:01:30.503
Of course Holly+ can -- thank you.

00:01:30.545 --> 00:01:33.131
(Applause)

00:01:33.173 --> 00:01:35.884
Of course, Holly+ can also
perform my own music,

00:01:35.925 --> 00:01:38.928
so here's an excerpt
from my own song, "Frontier."

00:01:39.554 --> 00:01:43.516
(Singing "Frontier" in overlapping voices)

00:01:48.563 --> 00:01:49.731
(Singing ends)

00:01:51.358 --> 00:01:54.527
Holly+ uses a process
called timbre transfer,

00:01:54.569 --> 00:01:57.572
where the timbre,
or sound quality of one sound,

00:01:57.572 --> 00:02:00.367
may be mapped onto
the performance of another.

00:02:01.534 --> 00:02:05.789
Timbre transfer is done by creating
a machine-learning model of a sound.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:07.749
In this case, it's my voice.

00:02:07.749 --> 00:02:12.212
So I recorded a wide variety of phrases
in my entire vocal range.

00:02:14.172 --> 00:02:18.635
To be clear, this version of Holly+
is reading notes from a score.

00:02:19.052 --> 00:02:23.264
So I'll also play you an example
of her singing in Spanish,

00:02:23.306 --> 00:02:25.475
“Bésame Mucho” by Velázquez.

00:02:26.226 --> 00:02:28.603
(Music: "Bésame Mucho")

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.242
(Music ends)

00:02:44.869 --> 00:02:46.037
Thank you.

00:02:46.079 --> 00:02:47.247
(Applause)

00:02:47.288 --> 00:02:49.249
I can't speak Spanish, by the way, so.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.003
Teaching an AI the sonic
properties of one sound

00:02:54.045 --> 00:02:56.256
in order to generate an entirely new sound

00:02:56.297 --> 00:02:58.425
is what I like to call "spawning."

00:02:59.092 --> 00:03:03.012
Spawning is what allows Holly+
to create a wide range of vocals

00:03:03.012 --> 00:03:04.389
that I didn't sing

00:03:04.431 --> 00:03:07.308
from a set of recorded phrases
that I did sing.

00:03:07.976 --> 00:03:12.063
I like to think of spawning
as a kind of 21st-century corollary

00:03:12.105 --> 00:03:14.441
to the musical tradition of sampling,

00:03:14.482 --> 00:03:18.528
which had a really big impact on both
music and intellectual property.

00:03:19.195 --> 00:03:23.491
But I think spawning is far more exciting
and potentially really weird.

00:03:23.908 --> 00:03:25.118
(Laughter)

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.245
So, for example, with sampling,

00:03:27.287 --> 00:03:33.168
usually you copy and remix a recording
by someone else to create something new.

00:03:33.835 --> 00:03:36.963
But with spawning,
you can perform as someone else

00:03:37.005 --> 00:03:39.132
based on trained information about them.

00:03:40.216 --> 00:03:44.429
And as an artist, this is making me
rethink my own past work

00:03:44.471 --> 00:03:45.889
as not only my archive

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.602
but potentially also I myself
could become reanimated with AI.

00:03:50.977 --> 00:03:52.479
This also opens up the question

00:03:52.479 --> 00:03:55.482
of how we deal with
a collective human archive

00:03:55.523 --> 00:03:57.609
if we can reanimate old media.

00:03:58.401 --> 00:04:00.153
It opens up really big ethical

00:04:00.195 --> 00:04:02.238
and intellectual property questions

00:04:02.238 --> 00:04:06.451
that require entirely new
conceptual and legal frameworks.

00:04:07.827 --> 00:04:10.538
One way that I like to think
about intellectual property

00:04:10.538 --> 00:04:12.207
I call identity play.

00:04:12.999 --> 00:04:15.835
So rather than limiting
the use of my voice,

00:04:15.877 --> 00:04:19.506
I'm creating instruments
to allow as many people as possible

00:04:19.506 --> 00:04:22.717
to create music with me, and even as me.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.638
That's why I've made versions
of Holly+ freely available

00:04:26.679 --> 00:04:28.515
for anyone to use online.

00:04:29.098 --> 00:04:31.976
If I can allow people to play with my IP,

00:04:32.018 --> 00:04:34.813
my digital identity,
my intellectual property,

00:04:34.813 --> 00:04:36.773
what might they come up with?

00:04:37.398 --> 00:04:41.319
Could someone else go on tour as me,
with my permission?

00:04:42.946 --> 00:04:46.783
Could I be in a thousand different
bands in multiple languages?

00:04:46.825 --> 00:04:48.701
And what would that even sound like?

00:04:49.661 --> 00:04:54.165
To be clear, musicians have been taught
to be really protective over our IP,

00:04:54.165 --> 00:04:55.959
and that's for really good reason.

00:04:56.459 --> 00:05:01.548
But I'm trying to think of ways to make
this new capability mutually beneficial.

00:05:01.589 --> 00:05:03.758
So to allow people to use my voice

00:05:03.758 --> 00:05:07.512
but still to maintain the ability
to approve certain derivative works.

00:05:08.513 --> 00:05:12.308
So I invite you to consider,
if given the opportunity,

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.602
who would you like to perform through?

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.731
And can you imagine someone
else performing you?

00:05:19.566 --> 00:05:20.775
With that in mind,

00:05:20.817 --> 00:05:23.987
I'd like to invite the incredible
musician Pher to the stage.

00:05:24.028 --> 00:05:29.200
(Applause)

00:05:29.242 --> 00:05:32.120
So today, Pher will be performing
his own song, "Murky."

00:05:32.787 --> 00:05:37.000
And with one microphone you'll hear
Pher's beautiful, natural voice.

00:05:37.375 --> 00:05:40.628
(Pher vocalizing)

00:05:41.421 --> 00:05:43.423
(Audience cheers)

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:45.341
And with this microphone,

00:05:45.383 --> 00:05:49.304
you'll hear a live version of Holly+
developed with Voctro Labs.

00:05:51.723 --> 00:05:55.226
(Pher vocalizing in Holly+ voice)

00:05:55.935 --> 00:06:00.481
(Laughter and applause)

00:06:01.024 --> 00:06:02.525
Take it away Pher.

00:06:05.111 --> 00:06:06.279
(Pher singing "Murky")

00:06:06.321 --> 00:06:10.325
It gets so murky

00:06:11.075 --> 00:06:14.495
Loving what you know

00:06:14.954 --> 00:06:20.168
But I, I keep on swimming,

00:06:20.668 --> 00:06:23.463
I'm swimming

00:06:23.463 --> 00:06:27.926
Further up the road

00:06:28.384 --> 00:06:30.762
See, the problem is,

00:06:30.803 --> 00:06:33.514
I don't know what to say

00:06:33.514 --> 00:06:36.643
When you come around

00:06:36.643 --> 00:06:38.895
Acting this way

00:06:39.312 --> 00:06:42.023
Oh oh

00:06:42.023 --> 00:06:45.944
And, yes, the truth is

00:06:45.985 --> 00:06:50.198
I show you every day

00:06:50.615 --> 00:06:54.911
Cause you love to stay

00:06:54.953 --> 00:06:56.329
(Applause)

00:06:56.329 --> 00:06:59.916
Living in all the pain

00:06:59.958 --> 00:07:04.796
And it gets so murky

00:07:04.796 --> 00:07:09.342
Loving what you know

00:07:09.384 --> 00:07:14.097
But I'll keep on swimming

00:07:14.097 --> 00:07:19.143
Further up the road

00:07:19.143 --> 00:07:21.604
And I've been calling out

00:07:21.604 --> 00:07:24.857
Calling out your name

00:07:24.899 --> 00:07:28.653
Deep down in my sleep

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:34.492
And I even memorized your face

00:07:34.492 --> 00:07:39.080
So ain't no leaving' me

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:43.292
And I just handle my biz

00:07:43.292 --> 00:07:47.588
And keep the train moving on

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:51.509
And you know what it is

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:55.304
Is just you that

00:07:55.346 --> 00:07:58.016
I'm choosing

00:07:58.057 --> 00:08:03.563
Cause it gets so murky

00:08:03.563 --> 00:08:09.027
Loving what you know

00:08:09.068 --> 00:08:11.529
And I'll

00:08:11.571 --> 00:08:15.158
I'll keep on swimming

00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:20.288
Further up the road

00:08:20.288 --> 00:08:25.752
And it gets so murky

00:08:25.793 --> 00:08:31.257
Loving what you know

00:08:31.299 --> 00:08:33.551
And I’ll,

00:08:33.593 --> 00:08:38.473
I'll keep on swimming

00:08:38.473 --> 00:08:44.062
Further up the road

00:08:44.062 --> 00:08:46.773
Swimming

00:08:46.773 --> 00:08:50.109
I'm swimming

00:08:50.109 --> 00:08:55.198
Further up the road

00:08:55.823 --> 00:08:59.118
And I'll keep on swimming

00:08:59.577 --> 00:09:02.538
Swimming

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:08.002
Further up the road

00:09:08.628 --> 00:09:13.382
(Vocalizing)

00:09:14.008 --> 00:09:20.139
(Cheers and applause)

00:09:20.139 --> 00:09:21.516
Holly Herndon: Thank you.

00:09:21.849 --> 00:09:22.975
Thank you.

